id: rust.tokio-semaphore
kind: rust
title: tokio::sync::Semaphore
aliases: semaphore, permit, bound concurrency
tags: async, concurrency, backpressure
questions:
  - how do you limit how many requests or jobs run at once?
invariant: A Semaphore issues a fixed number of permits; acquiring one gates entry and dropping the guard releases it.
answer_30s: A Semaphore caps concurrency: acquire a permit before the expensive work, and the RAII guard releases it on completion, error, panic or cancellation.
answer_2m: |
  The RAII part is what makes it safe: because the permit releases on drop,
  cancellation and early-return paths cannot leak a slot -- unlike a manual
  counter. Two placements: around a resource (bounding database connections,
  upstream calls, spawned tasks) and around admission (try_acquire for load
  shedding when the queue is already full). It composes with the bounded-channel
  pattern: the channel bounds queue depth, the semaphore bounds in-flight work,
  and together they are the standard worker-pool shape.
when_to_use:
  - bounding concurrent outbound calls, worker counts, or spawned task fan-out
avoid_when:
  - mutual exclusion (that is a mutex) or queueing (that is a channel)
minimal_example: |
  use std::sync::Arc;
  use tokio::sync::Semaphore;

  let limit = Arc::new(Semaphore::new(16));

  for job in jobs {
      let permit = Arc::clone(&limit).acquire_owned().await?; // waits at 16
      tokio::spawn(async move {
          let _permit = permit; // released on drop, even on panic
          handle(job).await
      });
  }

  // Load shedding instead of queueing:
  // if let Ok(p) = limit.clone().try_acquire_owned() { /* accept */ }
failure_modes:
  - acquiring a permit without holding the guard, so the slot is never released
  - a limit chosen without measuring downstream capacity, so the bottleneck just moves
follow_ups:
  - how do you shed load instead of queueing it?
related:
  - rust.bounded-concurrency
  - rust.tokio-spawn
  - design.semaphore
  - design.worker-pool
  - design.load-shedding
sources:
  - https://docs.rs/tokio/latest/tokio/sync/struct.Semaphore.html
